content.text:
I checked this carefully before responding, since it's a real regression risk if true. It isn't — here's the evidence:

**`.link` has never existed in `UserButton.module.css`.** The file only ever defined `.user`:
```css
.user {
  display: block;
  width: 100%;
  padding: var(--mantine-spacing-md);
  ...
}
```
`git log -p --all` on that file shows a single commit, and no `.link` selector anywhere in its history. So `classes.link` was always `undefined` at runtime — CSS Modules typing uses an index signature, so `classes.link` type-checks fine but there's no compiled class behind it.

I also ruled out the "literal string fallback" edge case some CSS-module loaders use (where an unrecognised key resolves to the key name itself, e.g. `"link"`, rather than `undefined`): even in that worst case, applying a literal `className="link"` couldn't have matched anything, since the only `.link` selector in the whole codebase lives in a *different* CSS module (`NavBarLinksGroup.module.css`), which compiles to its own scoped/hashed class name — not a global `.link` — and no global (non-module) stylesheet defines `.link` either.

So `className={classes.link}` was dead code from the start: not resetting link styles, not sizing the hit target, not doing anything. Dropping it when I switched to `Menu.Item component={Link}` didn't remove any real styling — there's nothing to restore. I'll leave it as is rather than reintroduce a no-op prop.

